# /etc/cron.d/tendermining — the schedule. doc/RUNBOOK.md §1, §1c.
#
# Two Monday jobs since 2026-08-18: the CYCLE at 07:00 (download, grade,
# train, predict, report — mails nobody) and the DELIVERY at 08:30 (render
# and send every customer from what the cycle wrote). Before that date it was
# one job at 08:15 doing both, and a cycle could not be run mid-week to see
# something without also mailing everyone. Note the fifth field: cron's
# day-of-week 1 is Monday (0 and 7 are both Sunday), which is NOT the same
# numbering as the Windows trigger's DaysOfWeek=2.
#
# CRON_TZ makes the schedule explicit rather than inherited. Without it the
# daemon uses the container's TZ, which is the same value today — but the two
# can drift apart the day someone sets TM_TZ for a deployment elsewhere, and a
# schedule that silently moves an hour is a bad way to find that out.
CRON_TZ=Europe/Bucharest

# CRON_TZ decides WHEN cron fires. It does not reach the job: cron hands the
# command a bare environment, so without the line below the cycle runs in UTC
# even though the image sets TZ. That matters because bulk.py and download.py
# choose the download window with date.today() — between midnight and 03:00
# local the container would ask TED for the wrong day. Verified: the first
# working test run stamped itself "17:10:02 UTC" while the container clock said
# 20:10 EEST. Keep this equal to the image's TZ.
TZ=Europe/Bucharest

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# cron gives a job almost no environment, so the state's location has to be
# stated here. These are the image's own ENV values (Dockerfile); if they ever
# disagree, the container is the one that is wrong.
TM_DATA_DIR=/data
TM_MODELS_DIR=/data/models
FASTEMBED_CACHE_PATH=/models_cache
HF_HOME=/models_cache/huggingface
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

# The redirect is not decoration. cron hands a job's output to the local mail
# transport, and a container has none — so without one the job runs, writes its
# logs under /data, and `docker compose logs scheduler` shows nothing at all.
# Verified the hard way: the first test of this file fired correctly, failed
# correctly, and reported absolutely nothing to the outside.
#
# It redirects to a FILE, not to the usual container trick of `> /proc/1/fd/1`.
# That trick needs the job to run as the same user as PID 1, and this job
# deliberately runs as `tm` while the daemon is root — so it fails with
# "Permission denied" before the script starts, which is a *worse* silence than
# the one it was meant to fix. Also verified the hard way. The scheduler service
# tails this file to stdout instead (docker-compose.yml), which has the side
# benefit of surviving a `docker logs` truncation.
#
# /data rather than $TM_DATA_DIR: the container path is fixed by the mount, and
# a redirect that lands in /logs/ because a variable was empty would take the
# evidence with it.
#
# m  h  dom mon dow  user  command
   0 7  *   *   1    tm    /app/docker/cycle.sh   >> /data/logs/cron.log 2>&1
   0 11 *   *   1    tm    /app/docker/deliver.sh >> /data/logs/cron.log 2>&1

# Four hours apart, and the gap is not a guess: the cycle takes 30–75
# minutes and may first wait up to an hour behind another heavy job on the
# lock. The delivery takes the same lock with the same wait, so a cycle that
# overruns 11:00 delays the mail rather than skipping it — and a cycle that
# died leaves last week's predictions, which deliver.py refuses (--max-age
# 1d) with a loud line in cron.log and no mail. The order of the two lines
# is the order of the day; nothing else enforces it.
#
# The delivery was 08:30 until 2026-08-27, ninety minutes after the cycle.
# Ninety minutes was enough while backplay finished by 08:00; it stopped
# being enough when backplay grew past twenty hours and held the lock
# straight through Monday morning. On 2026-08-24 the cycle waited its hour
# and died, the delivery waited its hour and died, and nobody was mailed that
# week — the run was repeated by hand on the Tuesday. The repair is the
# backplay line below, which now leaves Monday alone; this later hour is the
# margin on top of it, so a cycle that starts slow still has room to finish
# before the mail goes.

# The nightly backup (OPERATIONS.md 3): export always, off-site push when
# restic is configured, Sunday self-verification. 02:30 keeps clear of the
# Monday 07:00 cycle by four and a half hours — the export reads the database
# the app and cycle write, and WAL means readers never block, but a backup and
# a cycle racing for disk bandwidth on the same box is still worth the space.
  30 2  *   *   *    tm    /app/docker/nightly.sh >> /data/logs/cron.log 2>&1

# The backplay rejector (doc/PARAMETERS.md §10-11): measures the queue's
# candidate values — the program's own questions, one live knob per bucket —
# and may REJECT one, never promote. NIGHTLY 04:00 so a new benchmark label
# or Monday's store is measured the next night; cheap most nights because the
# job re-measures only when the evidence moved (benchmark blob, store files,
# champion fingerprint) and otherwise prints what it stood on and exits. 04:00
# is ninety minutes after the backup; it takes the heavy lock, so that
# separation is politeness and the lock is the guarantee.
#
# EVERY DAY BUT MONDAY (dow 0,2-6) since 2026-08-27. The clock separation
# above assumed backplay was a few hours' work: on 2026-08-20 it ran
# 04:00–08:09, and the Monday cycle three hours later found the lock free.
# By 2026-08-26 a single run took nearly twenty-one hours (04:00 to 00:48 the
# next morning) — longer than the gap to any other job, so a run starting
# Monday 04:00 is still holding the lock at Monday 19:00. Monday is therefore
# given back: Sunday's run ends around 01:00 Monday, nothing starts at 04:00,
# and the cycle at 07:00 has the machine to itself. The cost is one
# measurement night in seven, which this job already tolerates — it
# re-measures only when the evidence moved, so a skipped Monday is picked up
# on the Tuesday.
#
# If backplay ever passes ~21 hours again this stops working too: Sunday's run
# would still hold the lock at Monday 07:00. The duration is worth watching on
# its own account — it went from four hours to twenty-one inside one week.
   0 4  *   *   0,2-6 tm    /app/docker/backplay.sh >> /data/logs/cron.log 2>&1

# A cron.d file that does not end in a newline is silently ignored. This is it.
